{
  "index": 48,
  "text": "To the best of our knowledge, the present study is the largest sample multicenter real-world study which investigated the value of adjuvant immunotherapy after NCIT for locally advanced ESCC. The results indicated that adjuvant immunotherapy did not improve 2-year OS or DFS in patients with locally advanced ESCC following NCIT. Nevertheless, patients with residual tumor can derive benefit from adjuvant immunotherapy, which is line with CheckMate 577 trial.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

{
  "index": 57,
  "text": "In patients with locally advanced ESCC who undergo NCIT and radical surgery, adjuvant immunotherapy did not result in a significant survival benefit for the overall cohort. However, in exploratory subgroup analyses, patients with residual tumor after NCIT appeared to have a more favorable OS with adjuvant immunotherapy, a trend that is broadly consistent with the CheckMate 577 trial; given the limited number of events in these subgroups and the absence of a significant DFS difference, this observation should be regarded as hypothesis-generating and requires confirmation in adequately powered randomized trials before it can be considered practice-changing. Further investigation is needed to identify the optimal candidates for this treatment strategy.",
  "style": "Normal",
  "is_heading": false
}

